id: decisions/036-proof-reaches-outside
title: "What a proof must reach outside the graph"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'evidence-locus'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: evidence-locus
axis_gloss: "whether the warrant for a claim comes from inside the repository's own graph, or from something outside it"
poles:
  a: internal-self-sourcing
  b: external-verification
split: lone-exception
draft_status: full
leverage: 7.2
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(15) x split_weight(0.4) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  May a proof block be satisfied by pointing at other .yawn files in the same
  repo, or must proof reach something outside the graph (a commit, a deployment,
  a dated observation) before its status can rise above seed-backed?

why_it_matters: >
  128 of 194 files carry a proof block and 75 of those say seed-backed while
  citing only sibling .yawn files, so proof mostly means consistent with
  siblings; the one production-verified record is the only one with an external
  chain. Once answered, a bot that promotes a record or ranks a claim knows
  whether seed-backed counts as proof, and a child Yawn learns what it must
  fetch from outside the graph before calling anything proved.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    14 judgments sit at internal-self-sourcing (2 authored) and 1 at
    external-verification (0 authored). The repository asserts both directions on
    this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave means seed-backed as a real proof status or as no proof yet; and
    whether proof a single person can meet alone (J0264) is a principle of the
    protocol or only what was available.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: proof.source may consist wholly of sibling .yawn records. seed-backed is
      a valid terminal proof status; no external source is required at any status.
    cost: "None; status quo for 75 files."
    risk: "A record marked source-backed on a file no one else can open (references/scientific-frame.yawn) counts as proven; the canonical sentence's inspectable and replayable is not met."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "None can enforce this; the record would be a note in templates/proof.yawn that sibling citation suffices."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0012"
      - "J0062"
      - "J0107"
      - "J0264"
      - "J0273"
      - "J0463"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Every proof block must include at least one source outside the .yawn
      graph (a commit, URL, deployment id, or dated observation), or its status is
      capped at unproven.
    cost: "75 seed-backed files and all templates fail immediately; proof that a single person can meet alone (core/proof-and-boundary.yawn proof_can_be) becomes insufficient."
    risk: "Contradicts J0012, which refuses an external AI-detection score as authority; and forces external citations onto records whose proof is genuinely internal (a template, a node index)."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "A test in tests/ that every proof.source list contains a reachable non-.yawn entry."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0707"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: A proof block whose status is seed-backed or draft-backed may cite only
      sibling records. Any status above that (source-backed, production-verified,
      proved, passed) requires at least one externally checkable source: an https
      URL, a commit hash, a deployment id, or a dated named-observer observation. A
      source only the author can open does not count. External scores never count as
      authority (J0012 stands); external evidence does. The proof.status ladder is
      written into templates/proof.yawn.
    cost: "Write the ladder; downgrade or re-source references/scientific-frame.yawn and references/july-01-lock.yawn; no change to the 75 seed-backed files."
    risk: "Everything stays seed-backed forever because the higher rungs cost more; the ladder documents stagnation rather than curing it."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "A test in tests/ that parses proof.status and, for values above seed-backed/draft-backed, requires a proof.source entry matching https://, a 40-hex commit, a deployment id, or a dated observation, and rejects C:/ sources as not externally checkable."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0012"
      - "J0264"
      - "J0707"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.7
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    The lone exception is the strongest proof block in the repository:
    references/interception.yawn is behaviour-proven and proof-attached with
    commit, PR, deployment id, HTTP status and a dated browser observation, and it
    is the only file whose status reads production-verified. The internal-sourcing
    side is mostly revealed practice; its two authored judgments (J0012, and J0264
    on cheap solo proof) are both preserved by C. GROUND_TRUTH FACT 3 gives the
    12-value proof.status distribution and FACT 19 shows source-backed resting on
    a private file, which is the case C is designed to catch. Writing the
    exception down as a ladder is the smallest rule that makes the word proof mean
    something different at each rung.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "templates/proof.yawn names the proof.status ladder and what each rung requires."
    - "A test in tests/ fails when a file claims source-backed or above with no reachable external source, and passes."
    - "references/scientific-frame.yawn is either re-sourced to a reachable file or its status drops below source-backed."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A record claims production-verified while citing only sibling .yawn files and
    npm test stays green.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0012"
  - "J0062"
  - "J0107"
  - "J0264"
  - "J0273"
  - "J0463"
  - "J0707"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
